---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';

const path = '/compare/sequenzy-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = 'Sequenzy vs retired Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify recovery migration';
const desc = 'Assess Sequenzy for replacing retired Yotpo messaging while preserving consent records, recovery exclusions and any separate Reviews or Loyalty program.';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Sequenzy or Yotpo Email & SMS for cart recovery?', answer: 'Yotpo retired its Email and SMS products. Evaluate Sequenzy as a replacement candidate, rather than treating the retired sender as a current buying option. Its Shopify listing includes email/SMS recovery and purchase-based segmentation.' },
  { question: 'Does moving messaging require replacing Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty?', answer: 'Treat these as separate product decisions. Inventory the review content, reward rules and customer identifiers your recovery messages depend on; moving a sender does not establish that those dependencies move with it.' },
  { question: 'Can every old subscriber be imported as subscribed?', answer: 'No. Preserve channel-specific permission and suppression records. A customer row or past purchase does not establish permission to send SMS, and an email subscription must not be converted into SMS permission.' },
  { question: 'What should a Sequenzy trial prove?', answer: 'Use designated profiles to demonstrate checkout entry, purchase cancellation, channel exclusions and the handling of a loyalty reward already issued elsewhere. These are proposed acceptance tests, not results from a completed migration.' },
  { question: 'How should migration costs be compared?', answer: 'Quote the replacement email volume and SMS destinations, add implementation and data-reconciliation work, and account separately for any retained Reviews or Loyalty subscriptions. Do not compare a replacement quote to a retired messaging price.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Sequenzy vs retired Yotpo Email &amp; SMS</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">For Shopify teams rebuilding a former Yotpo recovery program, the decision is which sender can carry the required customer context forward. Keep the messaging replacement separate from decisions about reviews and rewards.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Replacement candidate', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Published Shopify email/SMS recovery and purchase-based segments.' }, { label: 'Historical sender', tool: 'Yotpo Email & SMS', reason: 'Retired; no current head-to-head sending trial.' }, { label: 'Migration priority', tool: 'Customer permissions', reason: 'Reconcile exclusions before importing an audience.' }]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Resolve the product identity before comparing features</h2>
        <p>Yotpo announced full Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. Its current end-of-life notice says the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. See the <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> and <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/integrations">decommissioning notice</a>, checked November 24, 2026. Old automation screenshots and historical prices cannot establish a current sending option.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/sequenzy">Sequenzy Shopify listing</a> describes store-data synchronization, email/SMS recovery and segments based on purchase history. These make it a relevant replacement candidate. A published feature list does not prove that an old reward identifier, permission timestamp or custom event will survive your import.</p>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Recovery dependency</th><th>Former Yotpo program</th><th>Sequenzy evaluation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Checkout entry</td><td>Recover the intended trigger from saved flow definitions or available records.</td><td>Demonstrate one eligible checkout entering once, with a usable recovery link.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Audience permission</td><td>Find channel consent, unsubscribes and complaint exclusions in retained exports or authoritative records.</td><td>Map each status explicitly; isolate rows whose permission cannot be established.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reviews and rewards</td><td>Identify messages that depended on a review asset or reward balance.</td><td>Demonstrate the required data path, or write a message that does not promise unavailable personalization.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Commercial comparison</td><td>Historical invoices describe past spend, not an available plan.</td><td>Price the replacement scope and any separate subscriptions you intend to keep.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Recover the program from records you actually have</h2>
        <p>Begin with the most recent export, saved templates, Shopify customer records and any migration documentation already retained. The decommissioned dashboard is not a dependable export path. Mark the source and date for each file; distinguish unavailable history from an empty audience. If a permission or suppression record is missing, seek an authoritative record before making the person eligible.</p>
        <p>Build a mapping with the old customer identifier, the Shopify identifier, email status, SMS status and exclusion reason. Resolve duplicate identities before assigning flows. Keep historical campaign engagement separate from purchase history: an old click does not tell you whether a checkout is still open today.</p>
        <h2>Proposed test: a reward holder abandons checkout</h2>
        <p>Suppose a designated test customer has an unused loyalty reward and starts a checkout. This is a proposed acceptance exercise, not a merchant result. First decide whether recovery should explain the existing reward, offer purchase assistance or issue a new incentive. Do not promise a live balance unless the retained loyalty product can supply it to the new message.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Record the test identity and intended reward state in the authoritative loyalty system. Confirm the same Shopify identity is represented in the replacement sender.</li>
          <li>Generate a designated checkout and inspect the event payload and message preview. Confirm that the product, link and any reward language match the intended customer.</li>
          <li>Complete the purchase before the pending reminder. Inspect the queue and event record to establish whether the reminder was cancelled; do not infer cancellation from an empty inbox alone.</li>
          <li>Repeat with an email-only subscriber and an unsubscribed profile. Record the eligible channel and exclusion reason rather than allowing a missing SMS status to become permission.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If reward data cannot be reliably reconciled, remove balance-specific language and offer help using an existing reward instead. This preserves a useful recovery message without inventing an integration between Sequenzy and a retained Yotpo product.</p>
        <h2>Check the cost of adding a second incentive</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $63 merchandise basket has a 37% gross margin before discounts, so its assumed fixed product cost is $39.69 and full-price gross profit is $23.31. A 20% recovery code reduces revenue by $12.60 to $50.40, leaving $10.71 before shipping, fees, messaging and returns. The discount does not reduce product cost.</p>
        <p>If a loyalty reward also applies, calculate its effect under the actual stacking rules before approving the message. Record who pays for the reward and whether it reduces the merchant's net receipts. Do not use the hypothetical $10.71 as a contribution forecast for a basket with unknown reward treatment.</p>
        <h2>Choose the replacement on operational evidence</h2>
        <p>Sequenzy is worth evaluating when you want recovery and later lifecycle messaging to use purchase context in one sender. Its <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/">product site</a> also describes AI-assisted drafting and event-driven flows. Ask the person who will maintain the program to revise an exclusion and preview the result during the trial; a capable editor is useful only if the team can operate the flow safely.</p>
        <p>Before activating recovery, identify every current sender, including any platform installed during the earlier Yotpo migration. Assign one owner to each reminder and resolve pending sends. Record how to pause the replacement and what approved fallback remains available; a retired sender cannot serve as the rollback plan.</p>
        <p>Judge the transition first on correct eligibility, intact checkout links and purchase exclusions. Once sending is stable, compare order-level net revenue and costs over a defined evaluation window. Platform-attributed orders alone do not establish incremental recovery, and this page makes no claim that a migration has improved results.</p>
        <p><a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/">Explore Sequenzy</a> with your recovered flow specification and data mapping. For a different starting point, see <a href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email">Sequenzy vs Shopify Email</a> when assessing an existing native recovery workflow.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sequenzy vs retired Yotpo Email & SMS FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
